<div class="page page-break">
    <div class="chapter-header">
        <div class="chapter-number">Chapter 04</div>
        <h2>Loyalty Packages &amp; Card Operations</h2>
        <p>Flexible package design and comprehensive card lifecycle management.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Loyalty Packages</h3>
    <p class="section-intro">
        Packages define what customers receive when they top up their loyalty card. Each package can include any combination of the five balance types, with location and country-level availability controls.
    </p>

    <p style="font-size: 10pt; color: var(--text-light); font-style: italic; margin-top: -5px;">
        Yes, five &mdash; not six. That's not a typo. From the customer's perspective, credit is just credit. How you handle VAT behind the scenes is your business decision, not theirs. Sapiens manages Credit and Credit-VAT as separate balances internally, but the customer sees one number. And if you're wondering: yes, you can switch between VAT-at-top-up and VAT-at-use at any time. And switch back. And switch again. The system handles it gracefully &mdash; even if you change your mind more often than you'd like to admit.
    </p>

    <table class="data-table">
        <tr>
            <th>Package Property</th>
            <th>Description</th>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Price</strong></td>
            <td>The amount the customer pays for this package</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Credit</strong></td>
            <td>Monetary credit added to the card balance</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Bonus</strong></td>
            <td>Promotional bonus added on top of credit</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Courtesy</strong></td>
            <td>Complimentary balance included in package</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Time</strong></td>
            <td>Duration-based play credit (in seconds) with configurable activation &mdash; either on purchase or on first play</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Tickets</strong></td>
            <td>Ticket balance included in the package</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Match Limits</strong></td>
            <td>Total match count or per-game match limits to control usage</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Location/Country Availability</strong></td>
            <td>Packages can be restricted to specific locations or countries, enabling regional pricing and offers</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <div class="value-prop">
        <div class="value-prop-title">Example Package: "Family Fun Pack"</div>
        <p>Price: 25.00 &rarr; Credit: 20.00 + Bonus: 8.00 + Time: 1800s (30 min unlimited play) + 50 Tickets. Available at all Spain locations. Time activates on first play. Fair-use rule: 1 game every 30 seconds (prevents rapid exploitation of unlimited play).</p>
    </div>

    <h4>Top-up Intelligence</h4>
    <p>This is one of those features that sounds small but changes everything at the counter.</p>

    <p>Imagine a promotion: <em>"Top up 20 EUR and get extra bonus."</em> A customer walks up and asks for 10 EUR. The employee adds 10 EUR. Then, before paying, the customer changes their mind: <em>"Actually, let's go for the 20 EUR promo."</em></p>

    <p>In most systems, the employee has to remove the 10 EUR, find the specific 20 EUR promotional package, and add that instead. In Sapiens: <strong>it doesn't matter.</strong> The employee just adds another 10 EUR. Sapiens automatically finds the best optimization for that customer &mdash; combining the two top-ups into whatever package or promotion gives the best result.</p>

    <p>But it goes further. If for <em>that specific customer</em>, based on their tier, their rules, their configuration, it's actually better to <strong>not</strong> apply the 20 EUR promo but something else entirely &mdash; Sapiens will do it. No one needs to notice. No one needs to decide.</p>

    <div class="callout" style="border-left-color: var(--warning);">
        <strong>Why this matters more than you think.</strong> Sometimes a promo gives just a couple of extra bonus. If it's not applied correctly, nobody notices &mdash; until the customer finishes their bonus and comes back complaining it's missing. What do you do then? Manually add bonus? That opens a security hole. The best way to fix a problem is to not have the problem in the first place. That's also at the core of our philosophy.
    </div>

    <h4>Time Balance &mdash; Smarter Than "Unlimited Play"</h4>

    <p>Time-based play sounds simple: buy 30 minutes, play as much as you want. But the edge cases are where most systems fail &mdash; and where customers complain.</p>

    <p><strong>Activation:</strong> You choose when time starts &mdash; at the moment of purchase, or on first play. If a customer buys time at 2 PM but doesn't start playing until 4 PM, should they lose 2 hours? You decide.</p>

    <p><strong>Scope:</strong> Time can be valid at specific locations, on specific games, or globally. Fully configurable.</p>

    <p><strong>Fair use:</strong> You can set a minimum interval between plays (e.g., 1 game every 30 seconds) to prevent rapid exploitation.</p>

    <p><strong>The credit protection problem:</strong> Imagine a customer is playing with time. They try a game that isn't covered by their time package. Most systems would silently charge their credit balance. The customer thinks it's covered by time &mdash; it's not. They find out later, and they're upset. Sapiens <strong>blocks the transaction entirely</strong>. The game won't start. Because allowing it would mislead the customer into thinking time was paying for it.</p>

    <div class="callout" style="border-left-color: var(--danger); background: #fdf2f2;">
        <strong>What happens when time expires?</strong> Many systems do this: time runs out, customer has credit on the card, the next game silently charges credit. The customer keeps playing, doesn't realize time ended, and later complains they didn't authorize credit use.<br><br>
        Sapiens does something different. <strong>The first transaction after time expires is always refused.</strong> Always. Even if the customer has credit. A clear message is shown: <em>"Your time has ended. From now on, normal credit will be used."</em> Only after this explicit notification will subsequent games use credit.<br><br>
        One refused game. Zero complaints. Zero disputes. Zero manual corrections.
    </div>

    <div class="page-footer">
        <span class="confidential">SapiensCore &bull; Confidential</span>
        <span>Page 7</span>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="page page-break">
    <div class="chapter-header">
        <div class="chapter-number">Chapter 06</div>
        <h2>Card Operations &amp; Counter Intelligence</h2>
        <p>What happens at the counter is where most systems fall apart. This is where Sapiens shines.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Multi-Card Operations in One Transaction</h3>

    <p>At the counter, reality is messy. A family walks up with three cards. One needs a top-up, one needs to be replaced, and they want to buy a new card for a friend. In most systems, that's three separate transactions, three separate payment flows, three chances for something to go wrong.</p>

    <p><strong>In Sapiens, it's one operation.</strong></p>

    <ul class="clean">
        <li><strong>Join multiple cards at once:</strong> Select several cards and merge them all into one in a single operation. All balances transfer. Full audit trail.</li>
        <li><strong>Top up multiple cards at once:</strong> Add the same package to every card (e.g., 10 EUR each), or different packages on different cards &mdash; all in one transaction, one payment.</li>
        <li><strong>Sell new cards in the same operation:</strong> Activate one or more new cards, top it up, and include it in the same batch with existing cards being topped up.</li>
        <li><strong>Add extra products:</strong> Food, drinks, merchandise &mdash; add them to the same transaction alongside card top-ups. One receipt. One payment.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>This isn't a convenience feature. It's a speed multiplier at the counter. Fewer transactions means shorter queues, faster service, and fewer errors.</p>

    <h3>Card Lifecycle Operations</h3>

    <p>Every operation is recorded with a complete audit trail: operator, location, timestamp, all balance changes, and tier assignments before and after.</p>

    <table class="data-table">
        <tr><th>Operation</th><th>Description</th></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Join</strong></td><td>Merge multiple cards into one. All balances transfer. Supports multi-card join in a single operation. Full chain traceability for fraud analysis.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Replace</strong></td><td>Swap a damaged or lost card with a new one. All balances and history transfer to the replacement.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Activate / Dismiss</strong></td><td>Bring cards into service or permanently deactivate them.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Reset</strong></td><td>Clear all balances and tier. Previous state is stored. Optional comment for audit documentation.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Reward / Redeem Return</strong></td><td>Ticket-to-product redemption at the prize counter, with full product tracking. Returns supported if needed.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Voucher</strong></td><td>Apply voucher codes to add balance.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Manual Ticket / Barcode Reward</strong></td><td>Manual ticket adjustments or barcode scans from ticket stations. Every adjustment tracked with operator and reason.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Discrepancy Resolution</strong></td><td>Resolve mismatches between what a game reports and what was actually dispensed. Later on you will discover what this actually is and how cool it is.</td></tr>
    </table>

    <div class="page-footer">
        <span class="confidential">SapiensCore &bull; Confidential</span>
        <span>Page 8</span>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="page page-break">
    <h3>Relevant History</h3>

    <p>Every loyalty card in Sapiens has a complete, reconstructable history. Not just "what happened" but "why it happened, who did it, and what the state was before and after."</p>

    <p>But the key word is <strong>Relevant</strong>. Here's what it means:</p>

    <p>When Card A is joined into Card B (with A as the primary), the history of Card B doesn't disappear. It becomes part of Card A's history &mdash; specifically, everything that happened on Card B from its last activation or sale until the moment it was joined. Because that history is <strong>relevant to the customer</strong>, not to the physical card.</p>

    <p>The customer doesn't care that the plastic changed. They care about their balance, their plays, their transactions. Relevant History follows the <em>customer's journey</em>, not the card's serial number. When cards are joined, you don't lose track of anything.</p>

    <p>This is <strong>time-sensitive</strong> &mdash; you can reconstruct the exact state at any point in time. And it supports scenarios that would break most systems:</p>

    <ul class="clean">
        <li>Card A is joined into Card B. Then the customer returns Card B, so you sell it again to someone else. The new customer tops it up, plays, and later joins it into Card C. Sapiens traces every step &mdash; the original join, the return, the re-sale, the new top-ups, and the final merge &mdash; with the correct balances at every stage.</li>
        <li>A customer disputes their balance. You reconstruct every credit movement, every play, every join, every top-up &mdash; and show them exactly where every cent went, at what time, by whom.</li>
        <li>A staff member is suspected of fraud. You pull every operation they performed across every card, with timestamps, amounts, and the card's state before and after each action.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="callout" style="border-left-color: var(--primary); background: #f0f1fa;">
        <strong>Relevant History isn't a log viewer.</strong> It's the customer's complete financial story &mdash; across any number of card merges, returns, re-sales, and transfers. It doesn't matter how many physical cards were involved. It answers: <em>"What is this customer's full history?"</em> &mdash; with evidence, not guesswork.
    </div>

    <h3>Transaction Engine &amp; Compliance</h3>

    <p>Every game play, top-up, reward, and transfer generates a transaction record. The engine enforces business rules in real time: consumption priority, tier-based discounts, VAT treatment, and balance validation.</p>

    <p>For a deep dive into how Sapiens handles VAT &mdash; including Credit-VAT, the feature that is probably saving you money right now without you knowing it &mdash; see <strong>Chapter 04: Credit-VAT</strong>.</p>

    <h3>VAT for External Systems</h3>
    <p>Sapiens doesn't just handle VAT for its own loyalty card transactions. It also manages VAT calculation and fiscal compliance for <strong>external payment systems like Nayax</strong>. When a Nayax transaction comes in, Sapiens applies the correct VAT treatment, generates the fiscal data, and creates the corresponding journal entry &mdash; automatically.</p>

    <h3>Credit Expiration</h3>
    <ul class="clean">
        <li>Configurable per organization: 6, 12, 18, 24, 30, or 36 months &mdash; or never</li>
        <li>Tracked per card, automated via batch processing</li>
        <li>Expired credit is accounted for correctly in both operational and fiscal reporting</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Tier-Based Discounts</h3>
    <p>When a customer's tier includes a discount, Sapiens applies it automatically during every transaction. The discount amount, original price, and tier reference are all recorded &mdash; full transparency for accounting and customer service.</p>

    <div class="page-footer">
        <span class="confidential">SapiensCore &bull; Confidential</span>
        <span>Page 9</span>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="page page-break">
    <div class="chapter-header">
        <div class="chapter-number">Chapter 07</div>
        <h2>Tickets, Prizes &amp; Product Rewards</h2>
        <p>From the game machine to the prize counter &mdash; every ticket tracked, every prize secured.</p>
    </div>

    <p class="section-intro">The ticket-to-prize flow is one of the most fraud-prone areas in any entertainment venue. Sapiens manages the entire lifecycle: how tickets are earned, how they're stored, how prizes are redeemed, and how every step is protected against manipulation.</p>

    <h3>Paper Tickets, Virtual Tickets, or Both</h3>

    <p>Sapiens supports <strong>both paper and virtual tickets</strong> &mdash; and gives you full control over which is used where. You can configure any combination you want:</p>

    <ul class="clean">
        <li>EMV (card) payments give virtual tickets, or paper, or nothing &mdash; your choice. <em>(Wait &mdash; virtual tickets on a Visa/Mastercard? Yes. We'll get to that. Keep reading.)</em></li>
        <li>Loyalty card payments give virtual tickets, paper, or both</li>
        <li>Configuration is <strong>per location, per game, per card</strong> &mdash; any combination</li>
        <li>Even when paying with paper tickets, Sapiens counts every ticket dispensed &mdash; so you always have real-time visibility of ticket output across all machines</li>
        <li>Real-time notifications when paper ticket stock runs low on a machine</li>
    </ul>

    <p style="font-size: 10pt; color: var(--text-light); font-style: italic;">
        We'll explore this further in the PulseBox chapter &mdash; the hardware that makes all of this possible.
    </p>

    <h3>The DW200 Ticket Station</h3>

    <p>The DW200 is the smart interface that sits on top of your existing ticket counting machine. It's not the ticket counter itself &mdash; you use whatever ticket eater hardware you already have (or buy one separately). The DW200 handles the intelligence: the card reader, the screen, the customer interaction, and the connection to Sapiens. Here's what it does:</p>

    <ul class="clean">
        <li><strong>Count &amp; convert:</strong> Customer inserts paper tickets, taps their card, virtual tickets are loaded instantly</li>
        <li><strong>Balance display:</strong> Customers can tap their card at any time &mdash; without inserting tickets &mdash; to see all their current balances</li>
        <li><strong>Multi-language, any time:</strong> The customer can switch language at any point &mdash; not just at the start. Most devices lock you into a language on the welcome screen. If you skipped it because you were in a hurry (or started inserting tickets), you're stuck. Sapiens lets you change language on any screen, and instantly shows the translation of the page you're currently on. Small detail, huge difference in a tourist-heavy venue.</li>
        <li><strong>Recovery codes:</strong> If the customer doesn't have a card, they can press <em>"I have no card"</em> on screen &mdash; or the system triggers it automatically after 90 seconds. A short, easy-to-remember recovery code is generated (e.g., <strong>A123</strong>). The customer takes it to the counter, buys a card, and the code is redeemed.</li>
        <li><strong>Auto-appear at counter:</strong> Recovery codes automatically show up on the counter system &mdash; the customer doesn't even need to remember the code. Staff see it and apply it.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="callout" style="border-left-color: var(--accent);">
        <strong>This is another feature that few &mdash; if anyone &mdash; offer.</strong> The seamless flow from "customer with paper tickets and no card" to "card purchased with tickets loaded" happens in under a minute, with zero manual data entry. The recovery code system bridges the gap between the Ticket Station and the counter without requiring the customer to do anything technical.
    </div>

    <h3>How Tickets Get on a Card &mdash; Summary</h3>
    <ul class="clean">
        <li><strong>Automatic game capture:</strong> When a game reports a win, virtual tickets are added directly to the card. No manual step.</li>
        <li><strong>Ticket Station (DW200):</strong> Paper tickets counted, converted to virtual, loaded on card tap.</li>
        <li><strong>Recovery codes:</strong> Short codes (A123) for cardless customers. Expire after 48 hours. Auto-appear at counter.</li>
        <li><strong>Discrepancy resolution:</strong> When a game reports a different count than what was dispensed, operators resolve the mismatch with full audit tracking.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Product Rewards &mdash; The Prize Counter</h3>
    <p>The prize counter is where customers exchange tickets for physical merchandise. Sapiens provides a complete product reward management system:</p>

    <div class="feature-grid">
        <div class="feature-item">
            <div class="feature-item-title">Product Catalog</div>
            Full product management with ticket pricing per item. Three-tier configuration: global product definition, country-level pricing, and location-level overrides. Stock tracking with desired levels and limits.
        </div>
        <div class="feature-item">
            <div class="feature-item-title">Smart Filtering</div>
            Staff can instantly see which prizes a specific card can afford, filter by category, search by name and browse what's available at that location. No guesswork, no manual calculations.
        </div>
        <div class="feature-item">
            <div class="feature-item-title">Redemption via Barcode or NFC</div>
            Products can be redeemed by scanning a barcode or tapping an NFC tag. Each redemption records every product, its ticket cost, and the operator who processed it.
        </div>
        <div class="feature-item">
            <div class="feature-item-title">Returns</div>
            Prize returns are supported with full audit trail. The original redemption is referenced, tickets are restored, and the return is linked to the original operation.
        </div>
    </div>

    <h3>Manual Ticket Controls</h3>
    <p>Sometimes staff need to manually add tickets &mdash; a machine malfunction, a customer complaint, a correction. This is one of the highest-risk operations for fraud, so Sapiens gives you granular control:</p>

    <ul class="clean">
        <li><strong>Per-location settings:</strong> Configure maximum tickets per operation and daily limits at each location</li>
        <li><strong>Per-employee overrides:</strong> Individual staff members can have their own limits &mdash; or be banned from manual ticket operations entirely at specific locations</li>
        <li><strong>Per-card daily limits:</strong> Cap how many manual tickets a single card can receive per day, per operator</li>
        <li><strong>Full audit trail:</strong> Every manual ticket operation records the operator, the amount, the reason (comment required), and the card's balance before and after</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Ticket Economics</h3>
    <p>Sapiens tracks every ticket dispensed by every game, in real time. This means you always know:</p>
    <ul class="clean">
        <li>Which games are over-dispensing tickets (costing you prizes)</li>
        <li>Which games are under-dispensing (frustrating customers and reducing engagement)</li>
        <li>The ratio between tickets earned and tickets redeemed &mdash; per game, per location, per period</li>
        <li>Whether your prize pricing makes sense relative to what games actually pay out</li>
    </ul>
    <p>This isn't a report you run once a month. It's live data. If a machine starts dispensing twice the expected tickets because of a hardware fault, you know immediately &mdash; not when your prize inventory mysteriously runs out.</p>

    <h3>Product Catalog &mdash; More Than a Price List</h3>
    <ul class="clean">
        <li><strong>Product images:</strong> Every prize can have a photo, displayed on the counter screen so staff and customers can browse visually</li>
        <li><strong>Multi-language names:</strong> Product names adapt to the customer's language &mdash; useful in international venues</li>
        <li><strong>Full redemption history per card:</strong> Every prize a card has ever redeemed is stored &mdash; product name, ticket cost, quantity, operator, timestamp. You can answer <em>"what prizes did this card take in the last 6 months?"</em> instantly</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="page-footer">
        <span class="confidential">SapiensCore &bull; Confidential</span>
        <span>Page 10</span>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="page page-break">
    <h3>NFC Product Tags &mdash; The End of Barcode Fraud</h3>

    <p>Barcodes are convenient. They're also incredibly easy to copy. A photo, a photocopy, a screenshot &mdash; and someone has a duplicate barcode that can be redeemed multiple times. For low-value prizes this is a nuisance. For high-value prizes, it's a real problem.</p>

    <p>Sapiens supports <strong>NFC tags on physical products</strong> as an alternative to barcodes. But not just any NFC tags.</p>

    <div class="callout" style="border-left-color: var(--primary); background: #f0f1fa;">
        <strong>SUN (Secure Unique NFC) protection.</strong> We use NFC tags with SUN authentication &mdash; the same cryptographic standard used in contactless credit cards. Each tag generates a unique, one-time authentication code on every scan. It cannot be cloned, photographed, or replicated. The tag can only be read by our system, and we know exactly how many times it has been scanned and by whom.
    </div>

    <p>Think of the Grua game where a customer wins a roll of tickets. Instead of having staff manually type how many tickets were won (trust-based, zero verification) or printing a barcode (which anyone could photograph and duplicate), the prize contains an NFC tag. The employee taps it &mdash; Sapiens validates the tag cryptographically, loads the tickets onto the card, and records the scan. If someone tries to use the same tag again, the system knows.</p>

    <p>No manual typing. No barcode scanning. No trust required. Just physics and cryptography.</p>

    <p>And the tags themselves? 0.60 EUR per tag. Or, we provide the technical specifications and you're free to buy wherever you want. There are cheaper NFC tags on the market, but they don't have SUN protection &mdash; which means they can be cloned. If you're trying to protect against theft, 0.60 EUR on a high-value prize or a high-ticket reward seems fair to us.</p>

    <h3>High-Value Prize Security</h3>

    <p>For valuable prizes &mdash; a PlayStation, a drone, an expensive speaker &mdash; tickets alone shouldn't be enough. Sapiens provides layered security for high-value redemptions:</p>

    <ul class="clean">
        <li><strong>Customer identification required:</strong> Above a configurable ticket threshold, the customer's information must be registered in the system before the exchange can proceed. Anonymous redemptions are blocked.</li>
        <li><strong>Real-time verification code:</strong> The system sends a one-time code to the customer's WhatsApp, email, or app. They must provide this code at the counter before the prize is released.</li>
        <li><strong>Mandatory NFC tag on specific products:</strong> You can require NFC tag authentication for specific high-value products, making barcode-based fraud impossible for those items. Look &mdash; we can't stop someone from physically walking out with a product under their jacket. We're software, not security guards. But every fraud vector that <em>can</em> be closed digitally, we close.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="highlight-box">
        <p><strong>This protects everyone.</strong> It protects <em>you</em> because employees can't easily steal high-value prizes &mdash; they'd need the customer's verification code. It protects <em>customers</em> because if they lose their card and someone finds it, that person can't walk up and empty 15,000 tickets on a PlayStation. They'd need the code that only the real customer receives.</p>
    </div>

    <p style="font-size: 10pt; color: var(--text-light); font-style: italic; margin-top: 15px;">
        Some of these security features are in final development. By the time you're ready to deploy, they'll be ready for you.
    </p>

