<div class="page page-break">
    <div class="chapter-header">
        <div class="chapter-number">Chapter 08</div>
        <h2>Payment Terminals &mdash; The App We Built</h2>
        <p>We didn't just install an app on a terminal. We wrote it. And we got it PCI certified.</p>
    </div>

    <p class="section-intro">
        This is where things get different. Once again.
    </p>

    <p>Most companies in this industry give you readers that only read their own proprietary RFID/NFC cards. You want to accept EMV bank cards too? That's a different terminal. A different provider. A different system.</p>

    <p>Some have recently taken an existing off-the-shelf terminal that accepts EMV cards and repurposed it for arcades. Sure, it works &mdash; but it's painfully obvious it wasn't designed for this. No status lights, oversized housing with extra room for nothing, generic UI that screams "I was built for retail."</p>

    <p><strong>We did something different.</strong> We wrote our own payment application from scratch. We designed a custom case with built-in LED indicators that we control from software. We deployed it on PAX iM25 and iM30 hardware. And we got the whole thing <strong>PCI certified</strong>.</p>

    <div class="callout" style="border-left-color: var(--danger); background: #fdf2f2;">
        <strong>PCI DSS Compliant. GDPR Compliant.</strong> No sensitive card data stored &mdash; ever. Certificate pinning on all communications. Daily auto-reboot per PCI regulations. This isn't a side project running on a terminal &mdash; it's a certified, production-grade payment application.
    </div>

    <p>And the actual card payment processing? We work with <strong>Planet</strong> &mdash; and we're proud to be their partner. You've probably seen Planet without knowing it: they handle tax-free refunds in most European airports and process payments for major retailers worldwide. A rock-solid partner. They handle the Visa/Mastercard tokenization and transaction processing. Because our app can charge against those tokens, that's why PCI certification was required on our side too.</p>

    <p>The result: Planet handles the payment rails. We handle everything else &mdash; the loyalty card integration, the game selection, the four operating modes, the UI, the error recovery, the remote management. We control everything that matters for your business. And that's why what follows is possible.</p>

    <h3>Four Modes, One App</h3>
    <p>A single universal APK serves every scenario. No per-customer builds. No different versions. One app, four modes. And if you think configuring all of this sounds complex &mdash; think again. More on that in the Game Configuration chapter.</p>

    <div class="page-footer">
        <span class="confidential">SapiensCore &bull; Confidential</span>
        <span>Page 11</span>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="page page-break">
    <h3>Mode 1: Arcade &mdash; Unattended, Single Game</h3>
    <p>Fixed price per play. Customer taps card (bank or loyalty or apple/google pay). Game starts. Supports tiered pricing (standard/VIP/Platinum &mdash; you choose the naming, this is to say up to 3 price levels can be shown on the screen).</p>

    <div class="callout" style="border-left-color: var(--danger); background: #fdf2f2;">
        <strong>Every iM25 is a self-service top-up totem.</strong> If a loyalty card doesn't have enough balance, the terminal doesn't just decline &mdash; it offers to top up right there. The customer adds credit with their bank card and keeps playing. No counter. No queue. No lost customer. There's also a direct top-up button on the idle screen for customers who just want to topup. With custom pricing per location, per game, per card, per tier &mdash; this is not a simple feature. <strong>This is a revenue multiplier on every machine on your floor.</strong> We go deep on this below &mdash; it deserves its own section.
    </div>

    <h3>Mode 2: Selector &mdash; Shared Reader, 2&ndash;8 Games</h3>
    <p>One terminal mounted between multiple machines. Customer selects their game from a visual grid (or arrow buttons for 2-game setups), sees the price, taps to pay. The correct machine is credited. Smart detection avoids unnecessary payment re-initialization when two games cost the same.</p>

    <h3>Mode 3: Attended &mdash; Staffed Counter</h3>
    <p>A multi-price grid for staff. Select a product, adjust quantity, tap card. This is where the <strong>quantity feature</strong> matters more than you'd think:</p>

    <div class="callout" style="border-left-color: var(--accent);">
        <strong>Why quantity matters.</strong> You pay a minimum fee per card transaction. If a customer has two kids, that's two plays. Without quantity adjustment, that's two separate transactions &mdash; two fees. Worse: what if the first transaction goes through but the second one gets declined? Now one kid plays and the other doesn't. With quantity, it's one transaction, one fee, both plays confirmed at once. Faster for the customer, cheaper for you, no awkward situations.
    </div>

    <p>Prices update in real time. No polling &mdash; no app restart.</p>

    <h3>Attended Games &mdash; Block, Unblock, Call Help</h3>

    <p>Attended games are different. Think train rides, water games, bumper cars &mdash; attractions where an employee helps kids get on and off. These games don't start with a pulse relay. They're activated manually by staff.</p>

    <p>But the payment terminal is still there. And here's the problem: while the employee is strapping kids into a train ride, <strong>another customer taps their card</strong>. Was it approved? Was it declined? Nobody saw the screen. The employee was busy. With Nayax, that transaction is&hellip; somewhere. Good luck finding it. With a physical button to block the reader? Buttons break, get stuck, get ignored and requires hardware implementation.</p>

    <div class="callout" style="border-left-color: var(--primary); background: #f0f1fa;">
        <strong>Sapiens: tap to block, tap to unblock.</strong> The employee taps their service card &mdash; the terminal blocks instantly, showing a clear multilingual message to customers. No payment can be processed. When the ride is done and they're ready for the next group, they tap again. Terminal unblocks. That simple.<br><br>
        The best way to fix a problem is to not have it in the first place.
    </div>

    <p>And it goes further. On low-traffic days, you don't need a person standing at every attraction. Leave the terminal blocked. When a customer wants to play, a <strong>"Call Help"</strong> button appears on the blocked screen. They tap it &mdash; a real-time notification is sent to staff. Someone walks over, unblocks, runs the ride. No wasted staff time on empty attractions. No lost revenue when a customer walks away because nobody was there.</p>

    <p>And this isn't limited to attended games. You can block <strong>any terminal, on any game</strong> &mdash; remotely. Machine needs maintenance? Block it. The screen shows a clear multilingual out-of-service message. No more handwritten signs, no more paper taped to the screen that falls off. When it's fixed, unblock &mdash; from your phone if you want. Done.</p>

    <h3>Mode 4: Kiosk &mdash; Two Sub-Modes</h3>

    <p><strong>Kiosk mode (with a kiosk):</strong> The terminal works alongside an external kiosk system. The kiosk handles the customer flow and injects payment requests to the terminal via Wifi (yes, no physical connection). The terminal processes, reports the result. Cancellation supported from the kiosk side. And here's the key: because the device reads <strong>both EMV bank cards and loyalty cards</strong>, you don't need a separate reader. One device handles everything &mdash; the kiosk uses it for payment <em>and</em> for customer card identification. One device, two functions, no extra hardware.</p>

    <p><strong>Kiosk-isolation mode (the device IS the kiosk):</strong> No external system needed. The terminal operates completely standalone as a self-service point. Customers can tap their loyalty card to <strong>check their balance</strong> or <strong>top up directly</strong> &mdash; select a package, pay with their bank card, done. A fully autonomous totem that you can place anywhere in the venue: near the entrance, in a waiting area, next to the prize counter.</p>

    <p>For kiosk setups, we usually recommend the <strong>PAX iM30</strong>. Bigger screen (5" vs 3.5"), and it supports <strong>card insert and swipe</strong> in addition to contactless. Why does that matter? At a kiosk, a customer might be paying for a 200 EUR reservation or a 100 EUR top-up &mdash; amounts where their bank may require a chip insert rather than contactless. No LEDs needed on the iM30 &mdash; the kiosk itself always has plenty of lights and the attention should go there, not on the reader. But it's your choice. Because &mdash; guess what &mdash; <strong>both devices run the same app</strong>. iM25 or iM30, same software, same capabilities, same configuration.</p>

    <h3>Automatic PIN Detection</h3>
    <p>If a card payment requires a PIN, the terminal detects it and shows the PIN entry screen automatically. No staff intervention, no confusion for the customer.</p>

    <div class="page-footer">
        <span class="confidential">SapiensCore &bull; Confidential</span>
