</div>

<div class="page page-break">
    <h3>The iM25 Custom Case &mdash; Designed and Produced by Us</h3>

    <p>Taking a payment terminal like the iM25 and writing a custom app for it &mdash; that's already unusual. But even with the best software, something was still missing.</p>

    <p>These devices are not built for entertainment venues. They're built for retail. They have a screen and a card reader &mdash; and that's it. No lights, no LED indicators, no visual feedback beyond the screen. In a dark arcade with music and flashing games, a small terminal with just a screen doesn't communicate enough. Is it ready? Did the payment go through? Is something wrong? Any special prices? Is it on promotion?.</p>

    <p>So we said: let's design a case. Not a bulky enclosure &mdash; something that <strong>seamlessly integrates</strong> with the device. Something you can add or not. Something you can decide on later, even after you've already deployed the terminals. It's an enhancement, not a requirement.</p>

    <div class="callout" style="border-left-color: var(--primary); background: #f0f1fa;">
        <strong>We didn't just write the software. We designed the hardware enclosure too.</strong>
    </div>

    <p>Here's what we built:</p>

    <ul class="clean">
        <li><strong>No extra holes required</strong> &mdash; the case uses the same mounting points as the iM25 itself. If you can install the terminal, you can install the case. No additional drilling, no modifications to the arcade machine</li>
        <li><strong>Built-in LED indicators</strong> &mdash; connected directly to the iM25, controlled by our app. Status, payment confirmation, error states &mdash; visible at a glance</li>
        <li><strong>30-degree inclination component</strong> &mdash; included with every unit. Provides the perfect viewing angle for customers and can be installed on any flat surface</li>
        <li><strong>We designed it. We produce it.</strong> This isn't a third-party case &mdash; it's engineered to work perfectly with our app and the iM25 hardware</li>
    </ul>

    <p style="font-size: 10pt; color: var(--text-light); font-style: italic;">
        And because we can't imagine building something that only works for us: if you already have iM25 terminals running on Android with a different app, you can use just the case with LEDs. We wrote a general-purpose mini controller and an Android SDK. It simply works &mdash; regardless of what app you're running on the terminal.
    </p>

    <p>Both terminals support <strong>portrait, landscape, and reversed orientation</strong> &mdash; configurable per device. The app adapts the entire UI automatically. Install the terminal however it fits best on your machine. The software follows.</p>

    <h3>Dual Card Detection</h3>

    <p>The terminal detects both EMV bank cards (Visa, Mastercard, Amex, Apple Pay, Google Pay) and RFID/NFC loyalty cards <strong>at the same time</strong>. Hardware-level NFC fires within milliseconds. The customer doesn't choose &mdash; they just tap whatever card they have, and the system knows what to do.</p>

    <h3>Top-Up at the Machine &mdash; This Changes Everything</h3>

    <div class="callout" style="border-left-color: var(--danger); background: #fdf2f2;">
        <strong>Every single iM25 is a totem.</strong> Not a payment terminal. A fully functional, self-service top-up point &mdash; on every game, across your entire floor.
    </div>

    <p>Customers can top up their loyalty card <strong>directly on the terminal</strong>, without visiting the counter. Two entry points:</p>

    <ul class="clean">
        <li><strong>Direct top-up:</strong> A top-up button is always available on the idle screen. The customer taps their loyalty card, sees the available packages, selects one, pays with their bank card. Done. Credit loaded. No queue, no counter, no waiting.</li>
        <li><strong>After insufficient balance:</strong> Customer taps their loyalty card to play but doesn't have enough credit. Instead of walking away, the terminal offers: <em>"Would you like to top up?"</em> They top up right there &mdash; and if you want, Sapiens can <strong>give them one free game right now</strong> as a reward for topping up at the machine. Top up 20 EUR at this game and play one on us. Configurable by location, by game, by customer, by top-up amount. You decide when it makes sense. But when it does &mdash; the customer tops up, the game starts immediately, and they just had the best experience of their day.</li>
    </ul>

    <h4>Custom Pricing Per Everything</h4>
    <p>The packages shown on the terminal are not a generic list. They are <strong>built dynamically by Sapiens</strong> based on:</p>

    <ul class="clean">
        <li><strong>Per location:</strong> Different venues can show different packages and prices</li>
        <li><strong>Per game:</strong> A premium game can show higher-value packages than a basic one</li>
        <li><strong>Per card / per customer:</strong> This specific card always tops up minimum 20 EUR? Show only 20 and 50. This customer is VIP? Show them the VIP packages with extra bonus. A new customer? Show the starter pack.</li>
        <li><strong>Per tier:</strong> Sapiens knows the customer's loyalty tier and can present tier-specific offers</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Multiple balance types are added in one operation: credit + bonus + courtesy + tickets. Full balance breakdown shown on screen so the customer sees exactly what they're getting.</p>

    <div class="callout" style="border-left-color: var(--warning);">
        <strong>Think about what this means for your business.</strong> You have 50 machines. Each one has an iM25. That's <strong>50 self-service top-up totems</strong> spread across your entire floor &mdash; each showing the right packages for the right customer at the right time.<br><br>
        A customer walks up, taps their loyalty card, sees they're short. In any other system, they walk to the counter, wait in line, top up, walk back. Maybe they don't bother. Maybe they leave.<br><br>
        With Sapiens, they top up in 10 seconds and keep playing. That's revenue you were losing. Every single day. On every single machine. And you didn't even need to build a totem &mdash; you already had 50 of them.
    </div>

    <div class="page-footer">
