<div class="page page-break">
    <h3>Every Transaction, Visible in Real Time</h3>

    <p>Every transaction &mdash; whether EMV bank card or loyalty card &mdash; is available <strong>in real time at the counter and on the PDA</strong> (more on the PDA later). This matters when things don't go as planned.</p>

    <p>A customer pays, Sapiens sends the pulses, but the game has a mechanical problem &mdash; a jammed coin mechanism, a stuck relay, something impossible to detect from software. The customer calls for help. Your staff opens the console &mdash; or their PDA &mdash; and <strong>the transaction is right there</strong>. They can see what happened, when, how much was paid, and decide what to do. Refund, replay, manual start &mdash; whatever makes sense. No guessing, no "let me check with the office," no asking the customer to come back later. And no more <em>"did they really insert the coins?"</em> &mdash; you can see it. Right there.</p>

    <h3>UI Customisation &amp; Time-Based Scheduling</h3>
    <p>The terminal's look and feel is fully configurable. Skins, colours, backgrounds &mdash; you control everything. We usually apply a <strong>different background per game type</strong> so the terminal visually matches the machine it's on: a space theme on a space shooter, bright colours on a kids' game. It makes the whole setup feel intentional, not generic.</p>
    <p>Skins can also <strong>change automatically by time of day</strong> &mdash; a lighter theme during family hours, a darker one in the evening. All managed remotely, per device or fleet-wide.</p>

    <h3>Screensaver &amp; Campaign Management</h3>
    <p>A default screensaver activates after a configurable idle timeout. But the real power is in <strong>campaign management</strong> &mdash; and the fact that when idle, you have <strong>the entire screen</strong> at your disposal:</p>
    <ul class="clean">
        <li>Multiple image campaigns with day-and-time scheduling</li>
        <li>Overlay text with position control (top, center, bottom) and animation (fade, pulse, slide)</li>
        <li>Separate images for portrait and landscape orientation</li>
        <li>QR codes on screensaver in configurable positions</li>
        <li>Impression tracking for analytics &mdash; know exactly how many times each campaign was displayed</li>
        <li>All managed by you, from the admin panel &mdash; no need to ask us. Upload images, schedule campaigns, check impressions. Entirely self-service.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Your terminals aren't just payment devices. When idle, they're <strong>advertising screens</strong>. Promote your own offers and upcoming events. Or sell the space &mdash; run sponsor campaigns, provide impression data to advertisers, and turn every idle terminal into a revenue stream. You have 50 terminals? That's 50 digital displays across your floor, all managed from one place.</p>

    <h3>Multi-Language</h3>
    <p>You choose a main language for the terminal. Other supported languages roll automatically on screen with fade animations &mdash; so the device feels dynamic and directly multilingual without the customer needing to do anything. Currently shipping with English, Spanish, Portuguese, French, German, and Italian &mdash; but any language can be added without any issue. It's just a translation file.</p>

    <h3>Remote Control &mdash; Everything, in Real Time</h3>
    <p>Every terminal is connected via WebSocket. No polling. Instant command delivery:</p>
    <ul class="clean">
        <li><strong>Update prices</strong> &mdash; change a price and it appears on every terminal instantly. No restart.</li>
        <li><strong>Block/unblock</strong> &mdash; take a terminal offline remotely, or via physical service card tap</li>
        <li><strong>Inject payments</strong> &mdash; push a payment request to a terminal from an external system (Kiosk mode)</li>
        <li><strong>Change WiFi credentials</strong> &mdash; remotely, while connected via Ethernet</li>
        <li><strong>Reboot</strong> &mdash; graceful remote reboot when needed</li>
        <li><strong>Config updates</strong> &mdash; game mappings, skins, timeouts, campaigns &mdash; all without touching the device</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="page-footer">
        <span class="confidential">SapiensCore &bull; Confidential</span>
        <span>Page 14</span>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="page page-break">
    <h3>Self-Healing &amp; Offline Operation</h3>

    <p>These terminals run 24/7 in unattended environments. They can't afford to crash and stay crashed.</p>

    <p>The app has a built-in monitoring system that continuously checks its own health. If something gets stuck, it recovers automatically &mdash; no human intervention, no waiting. If all else fails, it can be rebooted remotely from the admin panel or Sapiens just does it for you.</p>

    <p>Many features work offline, directly on the local network. Loyalty card transactions continue to work without internet &mdash; the NUC handles validation locally. In theory, you could even run EMV payments offline (the hardware supports it), but we don't really recommend it. For loyalty cards though? Absolutely &mdash; your customers keep playing regardless of what your ISP is doing.</p>

    <p style="font-style: italic; color: var(--text-light);">It was genuinely hard to build. But now &mdash; what a joy.</p>

    <h3>One App for Everybody</h3>

    <p>There is one version of the app. One. The same APK runs on every single terminal, for every single customer.</p>

    <p>We don't believe in "custom builds for customer X." It would be so much easier for us to say: <em>you're special, here's your special version.</em> But that approach is old-fashioned and unsafe. The moment you have 10 customers with 10 custom builds, every update becomes a risk, every bug fix is a project, and some customers end up stuck on old versions that nobody maintains anymore. And if you look around you right now &mdash; if you have a few shops that opened at different times &mdash; we'd bet whatever you want that they're not all running the exact same code. Exactly because of things like this.</p>

    <p>Instead, when a customer needs something different, we build a feature and give it to <strong>everybody</strong>. Every update reaches every device. Every improvement benefits every customer. That's harder for us. But it's so much better for you &mdash; because you're always running the latest, most tested, most secure version. No exceptions.</p>

    <h4>Provisioning via QR Code</h4>
    <p>Configuration is done via QR code &mdash; <strong>no app required</strong>, just a phone browser. You can prepare terminals in advance at your office and ship them to the venue, or configure them directly on-site &mdash; your call. Scan the QR code, select the location, select the game &mdash; Sapiens handles the rest: WiFi credentials, game configuration, pricing, everything is pushed to the device automatically.</p>

    <h3>A920 Pro &mdash; The Counter Terminal (and More)</h3>

    <p>The PAX A920 Pro is the payment terminal for your counter. It shares the same technical foundation as the iM25/iM30 app &mdash; same architecture, same security standards, same certification &mdash; but it's a dedicated application built for counter operations: card payments for top-ups, package sales, prize redemptions, and everything else your staff processes at the register.</p>

    <h4>How It Works with SapiensClient</h4>
    <p>When a staff member processes a sale on SapiensClient (the counter software), the payment amount is sent <strong>automatically to the A920 Pro via WiFi</strong>. No cable connection. The customer taps or inserts their card, payment is confirmed, and the result appears back in SapiensClient instantly.</p>

    <p>Each A920 Pro has a <strong>unique name within your entire business</strong> &mdash; not "POS 1" or "Terminal A." A real name you recognise globally like A123. The employee simply selects which device they want to use from SapiensClient, and Sapiens remembers their last selection to be faster next time. No need to configure POS mappings or assign terminals to registers. Anyone can use any device.</p>

    <h4>Transaction History &amp; Receipt Reprinting</h4>
    <p>Every A920 transaction appears in SapiensClient. If a customer comes back an hour later and asks for a copy of their card receipt &mdash; no problem. You can reprint it directly from SapiensClient, even if the A920 Pro that originally processed it is off, broken, or out of battery.</p>

    <h4>Queue Buster</h4>
    <p>But the A920 Pro isn't stuck behind the counter. It's portable. When the queue gets long, grab it, walk to the line, and start serving:</p>

    <ul class="clean">
        <li>Read the customer's loyalty card directly on the device</li>
        <li>Sell and activate a new card right there</li>
        <li>Process the EMV payment on the same device</li>
        <li>Done &mdash; the customer walks away with a topped-up card without ever reaching the counter</li>
    </ul>

    <p>There's no "queue mode" to activate. No special configuration. The A920 Pro is always ready for this. Just pick it up and go.</p>

