<!-- =====================================================================
     NUC — LOCAL SERVER INFRASTRUCTURE
     ====================================================================== -->
<div class="page page-break">
    <div class="chapter-header">
        <div class="chapter-number">Chapter 11</div>
        <h2>NUC &mdash; Local Server Infrastructure</h2>
        <p>The brain at every location. Industrial grade. Encrypted. Unstoppable.</p>
    </div>

    <p class="section-intro">
        Each venue runs a NUC &mdash; a compact local server that bridges on-site hardware and the cloud. It handles transaction buffering, loyalty card validation, device communication, and local operations when the internet is unavailable. But the way we've built it goes far beyond a simple server in a box.
    </p>

    <h3>Industrial Hardware with Power Protection</h3>

    <p>The NUC runs an <strong>industrial-grade SSD with power-loss protection</strong>. We provide a <strong>mandatory UPS</strong> (uninterruptible power supply) that is connected to the NUC not just for power &mdash; but also via data cable. This means Sapiens monitors power consumption in real time.</p>

    <ul class="clean">
        <li><strong>Instant power-loss notification:</strong> When electricity goes out, the NUC notifies Sapiens immediately. You know, in real time, which locations have lost power.</li>
        <li><strong>Battery monitoring:</strong> The NUC tracks UPS battery level. If power gets critically low, it <strong>shuts down safely</strong> &mdash; even if a firmware update was in progress. No corrupted data, no broken state.</li>
        <li><strong>Router protection:</strong> We suggest connecting your router to the same UPS. That way, internet stays alive on battery power too &mdash; long enough to sync everything before shutdown.</li>
        <li><strong>Voltage monitoring:</strong> We don't just monitor whether power is on or off &mdash; we monitor the <strong>actual voltage level</strong>. It happens more often than you'd think: electricity providers fluctuate voltage, and while you may not notice it, wrong voltage silently shortens the lifespan of your hardware. That game machine that keeps breaking for no apparent reason? It might be a voltage issue. We've already identified several cases where this was happening and passed the data to customers to raise with their electricity provider. The issue was fixed, the hardware stopped failing. We leave nothing on the table.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>And when power comes back? The NUC <strong>turns on automatically</strong>. No manual operation, no one needs to press a button, no one needs to be there. Power returns, NUC boots, syncs, and your location is back online.</p>

    <div class="callout" style="border-left-color: var(--primary); background: #f0f1fa;">
        <strong>If you think this is theoretical:</strong> in 2025, Spain experienced a massive nationwide blackout. We were operating. We had <strong>zero issues, zero errors, zero data loss</strong>. Every NUC across every location informed us, synced what it could, and shut down gracefully. When power returned, everything came back automatically &mdash; exactly where it left off.
    </div>

    <h3>Internet &amp; Networking</h3>

    <p>Our NUC could provide DHCP, routing, and more. But let's be honest: professional routers exist for a reason. And you probably don't have one &mdash; most venues run on consumer-grade hardware that was never designed for dozens of connected devices operating in real time.</p>

    <p>That's why we include in the activation cost a <strong>professional router and 4 access points</strong>. We'd rather provide it and let you decide whether to use it, than watch you struggle with hardware that isn't up to the job. Once you see what a properly configured network can do for device reliability and real-time performance &mdash; it's probably a no-brainer. More on networking later.</p>

    <h3>The Spare NUC &mdash; Disaster Recovery in Minutes</h3>

    <p>Safe hardware is necessary but not sufficient. Hardware can still break. Or be stolen. So we provide a <strong>spare NUC</strong> with every deployment &mdash; to be left off, in a safe place.</p>

    <p>If the worst happens: plug in the spare, connect a LAN cable, wait a few minutes. The system rebuilds itself. <strong>Every single operation is continuously synced to the cloud</strong>, so the spare NUC can reload everything up to the very last transaction you processed. You're back where you left off.</p>

    <p>This is also why, even though the system works perfectly offline, we strongly recommend a stable internet connection &mdash; it keeps the cloud copy up to date at all times.</p>

    <div class="page-footer">
        <span class="confidential">SapiensCore &bull; Confidential</span>
        <span>Page 16</span>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="page page-break">
    <h3>What If the NUC Is Stolen?</h3>

    <p>In a world where data protection is law &mdash; GDPR, customer privacy, payment data &mdash; you can't just put things behind a password and hope for the best.</p>

    <p>Our NUC runs a <strong>custom build of Ubuntu Pro</strong> &mdash; Canonical's enterprise-grade Linux with extended security maintenance, kernel live-patching, and compliance certifications (CIS, FIPS). Fully encrypted at the highest level. If someone walks out with your NUC, they have a brick. They cannot access the data. You can sleep well.</p>

    <h4>Physical Security &mdash; Layer by Layer</h4>

    <ul class="clean">
        <li><strong>USB monitoring:</strong> Sapiens monitors in real time, offline, anything connected to the NUC via USB. Something gets plugged in? Storage is disabled instantly &mdash; and more than disabled: the device is blocked at kernel level. You get notified. Sapiens can also remotely erase any storage device connected to the NUC.</li>
        <li><strong>Stolen device protocol:</strong> You can mark the device as stolen in the admin panel. The first time it comes online, it randomizes the encryption and <strong>hard deletes everything on it</strong>. Unrecoverable. But you don't even have to be the one to notice &mdash; the NUC is designed to operate 24/7 from a fixed location. If it detects a random change in IP or network location, it enters <strong>security mode automatically</strong> and requires manual intervention to resume. The system protects itself.</li>
        <li><strong>Decryption requires connectivity:</strong> If a reboot is needed (rare &mdash; but a cable gets unplugged, things happen), decryption only works online with Sapiens authentication. No internet, no unlock. And to be clear: unlocking doesn't mean you can connect to it. It means the system decrypts enough to operate and expose its web services &mdash; that's it. You can't plug in a keyboard and screen and get a terminal. You don't have access to it, and you don't need to.</li>
    </ul>

    <h4>The Absolute Worst Case</h4>

    <p>What if everything goes wrong at once? Electricity out, internet down, nothing works. We provide a <strong>military-grade USB key with a physical PIN selector</strong> &mdash; a special, one-time-use key paired to that specific NUC. Connect it, enter the PIN, and you can bring the system back. It's the break-glass option. We hope you never need it. But it exists because we believe in covering every scenario.</p>

    <h3>Zero-Trust Architecture</h3>

    <p>We do not provide anyone direct access to the NUC. Everything is encrypted at the highest level, and no one can see how &mdash; by design. Even internally, very few people have access. We follow a <strong>zero-trust policy</strong>: no user, no device, no system is trusted by default.</p>

    <h3>VPN &mdash; Already Included</h3>

    <p>All data between the NUC and Sapiens travels through our VPN. Already included &mdash; you don't need to configure anything, buy anything, or manage anything. It just works.</p>

    <p>And it's not any VPN. Our network is built on infrastructure that supports <strong>point-to-point encrypted connections</strong> across every node, with automatic key rotation, NAT traversal (works behind any firewall without port forwarding), and <strong>access control per device</strong> &mdash; each NUC can only reach what it's authorized to reach. Devices can communicate directly when needed (peer-to-peer), without routing through a central server, which means lower latency for real-time operations. Adding a new location? It's online in minutes. No VPN configurations, no firewall rules, no IT tickets.</p>

