<!-- =====================================================================
     GAME CONFIGURATION: SEPARATION OF CONCERNS
     ====================================================================== -->
<div class="page page-break">
    <div class="chapter-header">
        <div class="chapter-number">Chapter 09</div>
        <h2>Game Configuration &mdash; Why Ours Is Different</h2>
        <p>Technical, marketing, and operational settings are separated. Because they should be.</p>
    </div>

    <p class="section-intro">
        In most systems, everything about a game is configured in one place. Pulse settings, prices, screen design, device assignment &mdash; all mixed together. Move a reader from one machine to another? Reconfigure the pulses. Change a price? Hope you don't accidentally break the relay timing.
    </p>

    <p>That doesn't make sense. Because the pulse settings belong to the <em>game</em>, not the reader. The price belongs to the <em>business</em>, not the hardware. The screen design belongs to <em>marketing</em>, not the technician.</p>

    <p><strong>Sapiens separates these completely.</strong></p>

    <h3>Three Layers, Three Roles</h3>

    <table class="data-table">
        <tr><th>Layer</th><th>Who Configures It</th><th>What It Defines</th></tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Technical</strong></td>
            <td>Technical operations</td>
            <td>Pulse count, pulse duration, pulse interval, GPIO pins. <em>"This Grua Minion needs 2 pulses at 100ms."</em></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Marketing</strong></td>
            <td>Marketing / design</td>
            <td>Background image, colours, skin, screensaver campaign. <em>"The Minion game gets this background and these brand colours."</em></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Business</strong></td>
            <td>Operations / management</td>
            <td>Price, discounts, VIP pricing tiers. <em>"This game costs 1 EUR, VIP gets 0.80 EUR."</em></td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <p>Each layer is configured independently. They don't interfere with each other. The person setting up pulse timing never sees pricing. The person setting the price never touches relay settings.</p>

    <h3>Why This Matters More Than You Think</h3>

    <p>The idea behind all of this is simple: <strong>you cannot possibly have the best technicians at every location.</strong> It's expensive, and it doesn't scale. What you should have is the opposite: reduce the knowledge needed for installation and maintenance to a minimum, and centralise the expertise.</p>

    <p>Have one expert in your office who can monitor, intervene, and guide local people &mdash; even non-technicians &mdash; to operate. The person installing the terminal doesn't need to understand pulse timing or pricing tiers. They need to scan a QR code and pick a game from a list. That's it.</p>

    <h3>Plug and Play &mdash; Scan and Go</h3>

    <p>When an installer places a new iM25 on a machine, they don't configure anything on the device. They scan the QR code on the terminal &mdash; <strong>no app needed, just a phone browser</strong> &mdash; select the location and the game. That's it.</p>

    <p>Sapiens builds the complete configuration automatically: it joins the technical settings (from the game type), the marketing assets (from the design team), and the business rules (from operations) into one device config and pushes it to the terminal instantly.</p>

    <div class="callout" style="border-left-color: var(--accent);">
        <strong>Move a reader to a different game?</strong> Just say: <em>"Reader A &rarr; now has Game Football."</em> Sapiens changes the configuration and applies it instantly. The pulse settings change (because Football needs different pulses than Minion). The price changes. The screen design changes. Everything, in one action. No reconfiguration. No technician visit.
    </div>

    <h3>Game Authorisation &mdash; By Design, Not by Trust</h3>

    <p>Ever been in a situation where a game was moved from Location A to Location B, but nobody updated the configuration? Your reports now make no sense. Revenue is attributed to the wrong location. The data is there, but it's wrong &mdash; which is worse than not having it at all.</p>

    <p>In Sapiens, <strong>games are authorised per location</strong>. If a technician physically moves a game from A to B, it will not work in B until someone updates that change in Sapiens. By design. It just doesn't start.</p>

    <div class="callout" style="border-left-color: var(--warning);">
        <strong>You may think this is overkill.</strong> But consider: we support multiple companies at the same time. It happens that you own several companies and want the loyalty card to work across all of them. When a technician moves a game without updating the system, not only does reporting break &mdash; it's not even legally correct. Revenue is being recorded under the wrong entity. So we force the update. And if you forget? It just doesn't work &mdash; and the fix is one click away.
    </div>

    <div class="page-footer">
        <span class="confidential">SapiensCore &bull; Confidential</span>
        <span>Page 16</span>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="page page-break">
    <h3>Same for PulseBox</h3>

    <p>Add a new PulseBox? Same flow. Scan the QR code on the device, select location and game, and Sapiens applies the right configuration for that hardware model. If that specific game on that specific machine needs something different from the standard &mdash; and yes, we know: sometimes identical hardware doesn't mean identical configuration &mdash; you can override at the individual game level.</p>

    <h3>Remote Testing &mdash; From Your Phone</h3>

    <p>From the Sapiens admin console &mdash; <strong>even from your phone, no app required</strong> &mdash; you can:</p>

    <ul class="clean">
        <li><strong>Start a game</strong> &mdash; remotely trigger the game to verify the relay works</li>
        <li><strong>Dispense paper tickets</strong> &mdash; send a ticket payout command to test the ticket mechanism</li>
        <li><strong>Play with pins</strong> &mdash; send X pulses of Y duration at Z interval on PIN A. Full manual relay control for troubleshooting</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Trying is fast. Testing is fast. Understanding why something doesn't work is fast. You don't need to be in front of the machine. You don't need a technician on-site. You open your phone, tap a button, and the game across the country starts.</p>

    <h3>Safety Mechanisms</h3>

    <p>With this level of remote control, safety matters. Sapiens includes protection against accidental or malicious commands:</p>

    <ul class="clean">
        <li>Permission-based access &mdash; not everyone can trigger a remote game start</li>
        <li>Command logging &mdash; every remote action is recorded with operator, timestamp, and device</li>
        <li>Rate limiting &mdash; prevents command flooding</li>
        <li>Device-level validation &mdash; commands are verified against the device's current configuration before execution</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="highlight-box">
        <p><strong>The point is simple.</strong> In most systems, configuration is painful, moving hardware is a project, and testing requires being there. In Sapiens, configuration is automatic, moving hardware is one click, and testing is a button on your phone. This is what happens when technical, marketing, and operational concerns are properly separated &mdash; and the system is smart enough to assemble the right configuration on demand.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="page-footer">
        <span class="confidential">SapiensCore &bull; Confidential</span>
        <span>Page 17</span>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- =====================================================================
     PULSEBOX
     ====================================================================== -->
<div class="page page-break">
    <div class="chapter-header">
        <div class="chapter-number">Chapter 10</div>
        <h2>PulseBox</h2>
        <p>Custom IoT hardware that brings any arcade game into the digital payment ecosystem.</p>
    </div>

    <p class="section-intro">
        PulseBox is SapiensCore's proprietary arcade game controller &mdash; a compact ESP32-based device that connects traditional coin-operated arcade machines to the platform's digital payment infrastructure. It enables any game to accept loyalty card payments, mobile NFC/QR payments, and remote start commands.
    </p>

    <div class="stat-grid">
        <div class="stat-card">
            <div class="stat-number">ESP32</div>
            <div class="stat-label">VROOM-32 Chipset</div>
        </div>
        <div class="stat-card">
            <div class="stat-number">1-8</div>
            <div class="stat-label">Pulses per Game Start</div>
        </div>
        <div class="stat-card">
            <div class="stat-number">WiFi</div>
            <div class="stat-label">Connectivity</div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <h3>How PulseBox Works</h3>
    <div class="flow-diagram">
        <div class="flow-step">Customer Pays</div>
        <span class="flow-arrow">&rarr;</span>
        <div class="flow-step" style="background: var(--accent-dark);">Platform Validates</div>
        <span class="flow-arrow">&rarr;</span>
        <div class="flow-step">Command Sent to PulseBox</div>
        <span class="flow-arrow">&rarr;</span>
        <div class="flow-step" style="background: var(--success);">GPIO Relay Fires</div>
        <span class="flow-arrow">&rarr;</span>
        <div class="flow-step">Game Starts</div>
    </div>

    <p>PulseBox replaces the traditional coin mechanism's electrical signal with a digitally controlled relay. When a valid payment is confirmed &mdash; whether from a loyalty card swipe, a mobile QR payment, or a terminal transaction &mdash; SapiensCore sends a start command to the PulseBox via WebSocket. The PulseBox activates its GPIO relay, simulating the coin insertion signal that the game expects.</p>

    <h3>Technical Specifications</h3>
    <table class="data-table">
        <tr>
            <th>Feature</th>
            <th>Detail</th>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Chipset</strong></td>
            <td>ESP32-WROOM-32 module</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Relay Control</strong></td>
            <td>GPIO-driven relay for game start signal. Configurable pulse count (1 to 8 pulses per game start) to match different game requirements.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Coin Tracking</strong></td>
            <td>Monitors the game's original coin insertion input, tracking physical coin drops even when digital payment is the primary method.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Connectivity</strong></td>
            <td>WiFi with automatic reconnection. Maintains persistent WebSocket connection to daemon-core for real-time command receipt.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Firmware Management</strong></td>
            <td>Versioned firmware with OTA (over-the-air) update capability. Each version tracked in SapiensCore with supported/active status.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Security</strong></td>
            <td>Encrypted token authentication for all communications with the central platform.</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <div class="page-footer">
        <span class="confidential">SapiensCore &bull; Confidential</span>
        <span>Page 13</span>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="page page-break">
    <h3>PulseBox in the Venue</h3>

    <div class="value-prop">
        <div class="value-prop-title">Zero Game Modification Required</div>
        <p>PulseBox connects to the existing coin mechanism wiring of any arcade game. The game itself requires no software modification, no firmware update, and no manufacturer involvement. If the game has a coin slot, PulseBox can control it.</p>
    </div>

    <h4>Deployment Scenarios</h4>
    <div class="feature-grid">
        <div class="feature-item">
            <div class="feature-item-title">Loyalty Card Play</div>
            Customer taps their NFC loyalty card on a reader connected to the game. The platform validates the card, deducts the game price from the appropriate balance, and sends a start command to PulseBox.
        </div>
        <div class="feature-item">
            <div class="feature-item-title">QR Mobile Payment</div>
            Customer scans a QR code displayed on the game machine, pays via Stripe on their phone, and PulseBox starts the game automatically.
        </div>
        <div class="feature-item">
            <div class="feature-item-title">Terminal-Initiated Play</div>
            Customer pays at a PAX iM25/iM30 terminal mounted on the game. The terminal communicates the payment confirmation, and PulseBox receives the start command.
        </div>
        <div class="feature-item">
            <div class="feature-item-title">Coin Insertion Tracking</div>
            Even when customers insert physical coins, PulseBox records the event, enabling complete revenue tracking regardless of payment method.
        </div>
    </div>

    <h3>Real-Time Status via daemon-core</h3>
    <p>Every PulseBox maintains a persistent WebSocket connection to daemon-core (Chapter 22). This enables:</p>
    <ul class="clean">
        <li><strong>Online/Offline detection</strong> with sub-5-millisecond awareness across all locations</li>
        <li><strong>Instant command delivery</strong> &mdash; game start commands arrive in milliseconds, not seconds</li>
        <li><strong>Heartbeat monitoring</strong> with configurable intervals (DeviceCoolcodeHeartbeat tracking)</li>
        <li><strong>Push notifications</strong> to venue managers when a PulseBox goes offline</li>
        <li><strong>Remote diagnostics</strong> &mdash; query device status and firmware version without physical access</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="highlight-box success">
        <p><strong>Why PulseBox matters:</strong> PulseBox transforms any coin-operated arcade game into a connected, cashless-ready device &mdash; without touching the game's internal hardware or software. A venue with 100 arcade machines can be fully digitized in a single day of installation, with every game immediately accepting loyalty cards, mobile payments, and terminal transactions.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="page-footer">
        <span class="confidential">SapiensCore &bull; Confidential</span>
        <span>Page 14</span>
    </div>
</div>

