    </div>
</div>

<div class="page page-break">
    <div class="chapter-header">
        <div class="chapter-number">Chapter 21</div>
        <h2>Game Collection &amp; Cash Tracking</h2>
        <p>From the coin box to the bank &mdash; every euro is tracked, verified, and accounted for.</p>
    </div>

    <p class="section-intro">
        Cash collection from arcade machines is one of the biggest theft vectors in entertainment venues. SapiensCore provides a rigorous, multi-step workflow that separates duties, cross-verifies amounts, and generates legally compliant receipts &mdash; with full audit trails at every stage.
    </p>

    <h3>How It Works: Counter vs. Cash Separation</h3>
    <p>The system intentionally separates two distinct moments in the collection process:</p>

    <table class="data-table">
        <tr><th>Step</th><th>Who</th><th>What Happens</th></tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>1. Counter Reading</strong></td>
            <td>Collection staff</td>
            <td>Reads the mechanical counter on the arcade machine. The counter increments with every coin/credit inserted. This gives the <em>expected</em> revenue.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>2. Cash Counting</strong></td>
            <td>Cash handling staff</td>
            <td>Opens the coin box and physically counts the cash. This gives the <em>actual</em> revenue. This happens at a different time, often by a different person.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>3. Deviation Check</strong></td>
            <td>System (automatic)</td>
            <td>Calculates the difference between expected (counter) and actual (cash). Deviations above threshold are flagged.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>4. Accounting Approval</strong></td>
            <td>Accounting team</td>
            <td>Reviews flagged collections, approves or rejects with comments, sets the final accounting value.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>5. Fiscal &amp; ERP</strong></td>
            <td>System (automatic)</td>
            <td>Approved collections are sent to SII (tax authority) and Odoo (ERP) automatically. Receipts are generated.</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <div class="callout" style="border-left-color: var(--danger);">
        <strong>Why separate counter and cash?</strong> Because if the same person reads the counter and counts the cash, they can manipulate both numbers to match. By requiring two independent confirmations &mdash; often by different staff members &mdash; the system creates a natural check. If someone takes cash from the coin box, the counter reading will reveal the discrepancy.
    </div>

    <h3>Nayax Card Payment Deduction</h3>
    <p>When a game also accepts Nayax card payments, the system automatically subtracts card transactions from the expected cash amount. The formula is:</p>
    <p style="text-align:center; font-family: 'Courier New', monospace; background: var(--bg-light); padding: 10px; border-radius: 4px; margin: 10px 0;">
        Expected Cash = (Counter Diff &minus; Nayax Counter Diff) &times; Price Coefficient
    </p>
    <p>This prevents false discrepancies on machines with mixed payment methods.</p>

    <div class="page-footer">
        <span class="confidential">SapiensCore &bull; Confidential</span>
        <span>Page 36</span>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="page page-break">
    <h3>PDA &amp; OCR Counter Verification</h3>
    <p class="section-intro">
        Collection staff use a PDA (portable Android device) to perform game collections in the field. The PDA integrates directly with SapiensCore via API.
    </p>

    <div class="value-prop">
        <div class="value-prop-title">Photo + OCR Verification</div>
        <p>When collecting cash from a machine, the staff member takes a <strong>photo of the mechanical counter</strong> using the PDA camera. SapiensCore's OCR engine extracts the number from the image. If the staff member enters a different number than what the OCR detected, <strong>a notification is triggered</strong>. This prevents manual counter manipulation &mdash; the photo serves as tamper-proof evidence of what the counter actually showed.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Cash Movement Tracking</h3>
    <p>Beyond the collection itself, SapiensCore tracks where cash goes after it leaves the machine:</p>

    <ul class="clean">
        <li><strong>Collection point &rarr; safe:</strong> Track when cash is moved from the machine to the on-site safe</li>
        <li><strong>Safe &rarr; transport:</strong> Track when cash leaves the venue for bank deposit</li>
        <li><strong>Arrival verification:</strong> If cash doesn't arrive at its destination within the expected timeframe, an alert is generated</li>
        <li><strong>Full chain of custody:</strong> Every movement records who handled the cash, when, and the amount</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="callout" style="border-left-color: var(--warning);">
        <strong>Cash that doesn't arrive triggers an alert.</strong> If a collection is recorded as leaving the venue but the bank deposit doesn't match within the expected window, the system notifies management. This closes the gap between "collected" and "deposited" where cash historically goes missing.
    </div>

    <h3>Accounting Workflow</h3>
    <p>Once both counter and cash are confirmed, the collection enters the accounting queue:</p>

    <table class="data-table">
        <tr><th>Status</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Needs Approval</strong></td><td>Both counter and cash confirmed. Waiting for accounting review. Deviations highlighted.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Approved</strong></td><td>Accounting team reviewed and approved. Final accounting value set (can be adjusted with comment).</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Sent</strong></td><td>Exported to SII (tax) and Odoo (ERP). Legally compliant receipts generated automatically.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Rejected</strong></td><td>Accounting team flagged an issue. Rejection reason recorded. Triggers investigation.</td></tr>
    </table>

    <h3>Legally Compliant Receipts</h3>
    <p>Receipts are generated automatically with sequential numbering per location and year. Each receipt includes subtotal, VAT breakdown, and issue date &mdash; fully compliant with SII/fiscal requirements. No manual receipt creation needed.</p>

    <h3>Full Audit Trail</h3>
    <p>Every collection records: who created it, who confirmed the counter, who confirmed the cash, who approved accounting, and when each step happened. This multi-user trail makes it impossible to manipulate a collection without leaving evidence.</p>

    <div class="page-footer">
        <span class="confidential">SapiensCore &bull; Confidential</span>
        <span>Page 37</span>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="page page-break">
    <div class="chapter-header">
        <div class="chapter-number">Chapter 22</div>
        <h2>Loyalty Card Fraud Detection</h2>
        <p>Proven fraud investigation tools, battle-tested on real cases.</p>
    </div>

    <p class="section-intro">
        The loyalty card system is a frequent target for internal fraud. SapiensCore provides specialized tools for detecting and investigating card-related abuse &mdash; tools that have been used to identify and resolve real fraud cases, recovering significant revenue for venue operators.
    </p>

    <h4>Card Operation Pattern Analysis</h4>
    <p>SapiensCore analyzes the pattern of operations on loyalty cards to detect suspicious activity:</p>
    <ul class="clean">
        <li><strong>Unusual join frequency:</strong> Cards that are repeatedly merged may indicate balance laundering &mdash; moving stolen balances through multiple cards to obscure the origin</li>
        <li><strong>High-volume courtesy credits:</strong> Excessive complimentary balance additions may indicate staff abuse</li>
        <li><strong>Anomalous ticket balances:</strong> Ticket counts that grow faster than game outputs indicate manipulation</li>
        <li><strong>Zero-play aggregator cards:</strong> Cards with no game transactions but large ticket balances from joins &mdash; these are pure collection points</li>
    </ul>

    <h4>Recursive Join Chain Analysis</h4>
    <p>When two cards are joined (merged), the secondary card's balances transfer to the primary card. SapiensCore provides recursive chain analysis that traces the complete history of joins up to 5 levels deep:</p>

    <div class="value-prop">
        <div class="value-prop-title">Multi-Level Chain Tracing</div>
        <p>If Card A was joined into Card B, which was joined into Card C, which was joined into the target card &mdash; SapiensCore traces the entire chain: Target &larr; C &larr; B &larr; A. At each level, it shows: who performed the join, what balances were transferred, how many transactions the source card had, and whether the source card itself received joins. This reveals feeder networks where staff systematically funnel value into a single card.</p>
    </div>

    <h4>Ticket Station Recovery Code Abuse</h4>
    <p>When a customer inserts paper tickets into a Ticket Station but doesn't tap a card within 90 seconds, the machine prints a recovery barcode. This barcode can be redeemed at the cashier. SapiensCore monitors recovery code usage:</p>
    <ul class="clean">
        <li><strong>Volume per card:</strong> A legitimate customer needs at most one recovery code (before they have a card). Finding ~120 codes on one card is definitive proof of fraud.</li>
        <li><strong>Multiple codes in one operation:</strong> Staff scanning a batch of collected codes at once reveals systematic harvesting</li>
        <li><strong>Cross-staff tracking:</strong> Which staff member scanned each barcode, enabling identification of the person inflating tickets</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="page-footer">
        <span class="confidential">SapiensCore &bull; Confidential</span>
        <span>Page 38</span>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="page page-break">
    <h3>Fraud Investigation Reports</h3>
    <p>SapiensCore generates comprehensive fraud investigation reports that compile all relevant data about a suspected case into a single, presentation-ready document:</p>
    <ul class="clean">
        <li>Complete transaction timeline for the affected card(s)</li>
        <li>Card operation history with recursive join chain visualization</li>
        <li>All staff members who operated on the card, with operation counts</li>
        <li>Financial summary: real money paid vs. value extracted</li>
        <li>Prize redemption history with product details</li>
        <li>Daily activity pattern showing gaps and bursts</li>
        <li>Source card analysis (were they legitimate customer cards or shell cards?)</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="highlight-box primary">
        <p><strong>Proven in practice.</strong> SapiensCore's fraud detection tools have been used to identify real cases of employee theft &mdash; including a loyalty card bonus consolidation scheme spanning 3+ years and 7 staff members across two systems, and a ticket inflation scheme involving ~120 harvested recovery barcodes scanned onto a single card. In both cases, the investigation produced court-ready documentation with full evidence chains.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Cookie-Based User Matching</h3>
    <p>For online transactions, SapiensCore uses browser cookie tracking to link transactions to user identities across sessions:</p>
    <ul class="clean">
        <li>Identification of users making multiple chargebacks under different names</li>
        <li>Detection of repeat offenders creating new accounts</li>
        <li>Attribution of suspicious transactions to known user profiles</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Merchant Warning System</h3>
    <p>Proactive alerts for merchant account health:</p>
    <ul class="clean">
        <li>Bank-level warnings about chargeback ratios approaching thresholds</li>
        <li>Stripe risk level monitoring with automated escalation</li>
        <li>Historical tracking of all merchant warnings with resolution status</li>
    </ul>

