<!-- =====================================================================
     PAGE 38-39: REAL-TIME MONITORING (DAEMON-CORE)
     ====================================================================== -->
<div class="page page-break">
    <div class="chapter-header">
        <div class="chapter-number">Chapter 22</div>
        <h2>Real-Time Device Monitoring</h2>
        <p>daemon-core: centralized, sub-5ms monitoring across every location.</p>
    </div>

    <p class="section-intro">
        daemon-core is SapiensCore's real-time device monitoring infrastructure. It maintains persistent WebSocket connections to every PulseBox, payment terminal, and NUC across all locations, providing instant awareness of device status and enabling immediate command dispatch.
    </p>

    <h3>Architecture</h3>
    <div class="flow-diagram">
        <div class="flow-step">PulseBox / Terminal</div>
        <span class="flow-arrow">&rarr;</span>
        <div class="flow-step" style="background: var(--accent-dark);">WebSocket</div>
        <span class="flow-arrow">&rarr;</span>
        <div class="flow-step">daemon-core (Cloudflare)</div>
        <span class="flow-arrow">&rarr;</span>
        <div class="flow-step" style="background: var(--success);">Admin Dashboard</div>
    </div>

    <p>daemon-core runs on Cloudflare's edge infrastructure, providing low-latency WebSocket connections for devices across geographically distributed venues. Each device maintains a persistent connection, and daemon-core tracks the real-time status of every connected device.</p>

    <h3>Monitoring Capabilities</h3>
    <table class="data-table">
        <tr>
            <th>Capability</th>
            <th>Detail</th>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Online/Offline Detection</strong></td>
            <td>Sub-5ms awareness when a device goes online or offline. No polling &mdash; instant event-driven notification.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Device Types Monitored</strong></td>
            <td>PulseBox (game controllers), PAX payment terminals, NUC local servers, Nayax vending terminals</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Location-Level Health</strong></td>
            <td>Aggregate view showing device health per location: how many online, how many offline, recent status changes.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Push Notifications</strong></td>
            <td>Browser push notifications alert managers when devices go offline. Configurable per user and per device type.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Command Dispatch</strong></td>
            <td>Send commands to devices in real time: game start signals, configuration updates, diagnostic requests.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Heartbeat Monitoring</strong></td>
            <td>Track device heartbeats with configurable intervals. Detect devices that are connected but unresponsive.</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <div class="highlight-box">
        <p><strong>Why WebSocket?</strong> Traditional polling-based monitoring introduces latency (typically 30-60 seconds) and wastes bandwidth. daemon-core's WebSocket architecture provides instant bidirectional communication, meaning a venue manager knows about a device going offline within milliseconds &mdash; before a customer even notices.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="page-footer">
        <span class="confidential">SapiensCore &bull; Confidential</span>
        <span>Page 38</span>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="page page-break">
    <h3>Admin Monitoring Dashboard</h3>
    <p>The device monitoring dashboard in the SapiensCore admin provides a real-time view of all connected devices:</p>

    <div class="feature-grid">
        <div class="feature-item">
            <div class="feature-item-title">Fleet Overview</div>
            At-a-glance view of all devices across all locations. Color-coded status indicators show online (green), offline (red), and warning (amber) states.
        </div>
        <div class="feature-item">
            <div class="feature-item-title">Location Drill-Down</div>
            Click into any location to see detailed status for every device at that venue. Identify problem areas quickly.
        </div>
        <div class="feature-item">
            <div class="feature-item-title">Device Detail View</div>
            View individual device history, firmware version, configuration, and recent command activity.
        </div>
        <div class="feature-item">
            <div class="feature-item-title">Permission-Based Access</div>
            Monitoring access respects the user permission hierarchy. Location managers see only their locations; organization admins see all locations.
        </div>
    </div>

    <h3>NUC System Metrics</h3>
    <p>Each NUC (local server) reports system health metrics to daemon-core:</p>
    <ul class="clean">
        <li>CPU utilization and temperature</li>
        <li>Memory usage and availability</li>
        <li>Disk space and I/O performance</li>
        <li>Network connectivity status</li>
        <li>Running service health</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Command Queue</h3>
    <p>daemon-core includes a command queue system for dispatching instructions to devices:</p>
    <ul class="clean">
        <li><strong>Game start commands</strong> sent to PulseBox units after payment confirmation</li>
        <li><strong>Configuration updates</strong> pushed to devices without physical access</li>
        <li><strong>Diagnostic requests</strong> for troubleshooting device issues remotely</li>
        <li><strong>Firmware update triggers</strong> for managed OTA deployment</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="value-prop">
        <div class="value-prop-title">Business Impact</div>
        <p>With daemon-core, a venue operator managing 40 locations and hundreds of devices can identify and respond to issues in real time. A PulseBox going offline at a busy venue on a Saturday afternoon triggers an immediate notification &mdash; the manager can dispatch a technician before customers start complaining about a broken game.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="page-footer">
        <span class="confidential">SapiensCore &bull; Confidential</span>
        <span>Page 39</span>
    </div>
</div>

